WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.460
Welcome back. I'm Alex Lyon. This is lesson 3 of 7 in the course. In this lesson

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:09.240
I want to give a little warning, a little
heads up. A lot of people give pushback

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:13.440
on this particular strategy that you're
going to see where I talk about using

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:18.210
plain English, plain language to express
yourself. It's one of the most valuable

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:23.550
ways to make your messages more clear,
more concise, and more confident and yet

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:27.630
some people resist. They want to keep in
the sophisticated, complicated language.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:32.189
So, if you feel that you're in that crowd,
if you're one of those people that

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:35.880
doesn't want to do that, then check
your self talk. Figure out what it is

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:39.930
that's going on in your mind and address
it because, again, all the tips in the

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:43.950
world are not going to change the way
you ultimately communicate unless you

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:47.670
figure out what's happening internally
that's driving your communication. So

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:48.690
let's jump right in.

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:57.860
In our last lesson we looked at how to
form a concise overall message. In lesson

00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:01.980
three, will look at concise
sentences. Concise tip number three is

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:06.869
use short sentences. Oliver Wendell
Holmes, a well-known judge from about a

00:01:06.869 --> 00:01:10.830
hundred years ago, said, "Speak clearly if
you speak at all; carved every word

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:16.049
before you let it fall." It's a very poetic
way to say, you want to work on short

00:01:16.049 --> 00:01:21.540
sentences and express yourself concisely. Short sentences pop. They sound much more

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:27.330
confident, more concrete, and much more
memorable than long-winded sentences.

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:31.770
They sound like news headlines, topic
sentences in paragraphs, items in a

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:36.540
bulleted list, and one line jokes. A lot
of times people will develop a nice

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:41.640
clean, crisp way of communicating and it
sounds much more confident than

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:46.229
long-winded demanding sentences. So, you want to use as few words as possible.

00:01:46.229 --> 00:01:52.979
Strategy number one. Shave words. Comedian Jerry Seinfeld talks about how longer

00:01:52.979 --> 00:01:56.790
jokes are very hard to make funny and
their much funnier when you shorten

00:01:56.790 --> 00:02:00.689
the jokes and make them pop. He was
talking about the length of jokes and he

00:02:00.689 --> 00:02:04.979
said, "If it's too long, just a split second
too long, you will shave letters off of

00:02:04.979 --> 00:02:10.259
words. You count syllables to get it just
right. It's more like songwriting." When he

00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:13.650
says songwriting he is referring to how 
songwriters have to fit

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:17.340
their words into certain beats. And
they will have to come up with creative

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:22.560
ways to express themselves in very few
words. So Seinfeld has a joke about Pop-

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:26.760
Tarts where he really practices what he
preaches. He says about Pop-Tarts, "They

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:30.629
can't go stale cause they were never
fresh." Which is hilarious. But it's

00:02:30.629 --> 00:02:34.319
amazing how we can communicate that and in so few words. And there's not a single

00:02:34.319 --> 00:02:39.120
extra letter in this punch line. They
can't go stale cause they were never

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:44.519
fresh. He doesn't say they will never go
stale or they can never go stale. That

00:02:44.519 --> 00:02:49.620
would be too many words. You can't go
stale cause instead of because they

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:53.340
were never fresh. There's not, I can't
think of anything that I would shave off

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:55.920
of this and it's much funnier because of
it.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.859
The lesson here is you want to get rid
of the clutter so your sentences sound

00:02:58.859 --> 00:03:01.230
more concise and more confident.

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:06.000
So, which sentence sticks with you. Look
at two samples about voting. "If you are

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.659
bored and disgusted by politics and
don't bother to vote, you're in effect

00:03:09.659 --> 00:03:13.560
voting for the entrenched establishments
of the two major parties, who please, rest

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:17.879
assured are not dumb and who are
keenly aware that is in their interests

00:03:17.879 --> 00:03:21.120
to keep you disgusted and bored and
cynical and to give you every possible

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:26.040
reason to stay at home doing one hitters
and watching MTV on primary day." That's a

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:31.500
very long sentence. It's going to be difficult to remember what the message is here.

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:38.099
Here's B "if you don't vote you don't
matter." Which was said by Sean Penn in a

00:03:38.099 --> 00:03:40.799
movie about a politician from the South.

00:03:40.799 --> 00:03:44.609
If you're asking me which line sticks? If
you don't vote you don't matter stuck

00:03:44.609 --> 00:03:48.989
with me for years after I saw that movie
one time. Now, I don't know if the

00:03:48.989 --> 00:03:51.840
politician he was playing actually said
that or if the people who wrote the

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:55.680
movie just crafted it. But it's a
well-crafted sentence and again there's

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:59.970
not a single extra letter or word in
that sentence. That's why it sound so

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:04.470
confident, whether you agree or disagree
with the sentiment, it sounds confident and

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:09.329
it really pops. So, let's shave the
clutter to make this sound like a

00:04:09.329 --> 00:04:12.720
headline. A few things came up,
complications I guess you could call

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:16.139
them, that are going to cause some
unforeseen delays and I'm not sure if we can get it

00:04:16.139 --> 00:04:19.560
all done by Wednesday--now we might--I'm just saying that we're going to have to

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:22.349
see what happens and play it by ear.

00:04:22.349 --> 00:04:27.900
How could we shave this clutter to make
it sound much more like a news headline?

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:33.690
If it were me, I would do it this way. I'm not sure we get all done by Wednesday. Now we

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:36.930
might. You could even reword it slightly
I'm not sure we can get it all done by Wednesday

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:39.300
because of delays.

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:43.380
Is there anything else we could shave
from the sentence? Shave a few more words

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:48.270
like Seinfeld? What would he do? I think
probably would take the extra few words

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:53.310
out that he could and that would make it
sound even more concise and confident.

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:57.330
Let's try another one. Let's shave the
clutter. What do you recommend we do when

00:04:57.330 --> 00:04:58.620
we visit Rhode Island?

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:03.719
Well, let's see. I'd say probably the most
amazing place, I mean it's really amazing

00:05:03.719 --> 00:05:07.620
as long as the weather is good, is the
beach in Rhode Island, because that's what

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:10.620
we're known for and it's not called the
Ocean State for no reason.

00:05:11.849 --> 00:05:15.810
So, this is a pretty long answer for a
very short question. What do you

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:19.440
recommend we do? And if it were me, I
would shorten this all the way down to

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:24.570
get rid of almost everything and I would
say, The most amazing place is the beach.

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:31.680
Can we shave this even further? Well we
might be able to but you don't want to

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:35.639
overcorrect and shave it down to single
words. I think if you ask them this

00:05:35.639 --> 00:05:40.949
question and they answered, the beach, they might sound a little too clipped and

00:05:40.949 --> 00:05:46.199
little dismissive. So you make sure you
form full thoughts, full sentences and

00:05:46.199 --> 00:05:48.389
you're going to be on safe ground.

00:05:48.389 --> 00:05:53.940
Strategy number two. Use plain English. A
lot of people perhaps to sound smart,

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:58.919
perhaps because they love language, will
dress up what they say in unnecessarily

00:05:58.919 --> 00:06:04.469
complicated vocabulary. So you want to
avoid those kinds of bigger words

00:06:04.469 --> 00:06:09.210
because they don't necessarily add a lot
of value. Utilize. If you look it up, it just

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:14.010
means you use. Problematical just means
problem. Academia is another way to say

00:06:14.010 --> 00:06:18.210
college. You want to use plain language
whenever you can because every once in a

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:22.110
while, they'll be a thought you can't
possibly express plainly and you want to

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:27.060
leave room in your sentences for that
more sophisticated idea. And to do so you

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:30.750
want to simplify everything else that
you can into very plain English. I

00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:34.740
actually have heard a lot of people
coming up through grad school use the

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:37.320
word problematical and I just never
understood

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:42.090
why they would use such a demanding word. So, don't use flowery language when simple

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:46.320
language will do. Say it in plain English.
Contemplate. How would you say this word,

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:52.350
contemplate? How would you translate that?
I would say think. Commence. Is another

00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:58.320
fancy way to say start. And, Terminate is
a fancy way to say end. Now you don't

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:02.970
have to do this in every single case but
in general, to be concise and to make to

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:08.220
make nice concise sentences, you want to use plain English. Let's say this one in

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:11.820
plain English. I was reflecting on my
position and have altered my perspective on

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:18.300
the matter. What's the person really
saying? I think they're saying, I changed

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:22.020
my mind. And that's how you want to
express yourself. And there's no way to

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:28.800
misunderstand a nice plainly worded
sentence. So use short sentences with as

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:32.910
few words as possible. You take-away
strategies are to shave the clutter like

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:36.810
Seinfeld and other people who really
work on compressing those sentences. And

00:07:36.810 --> 00:07:42.510
use plain English whenever you can. You
take-away task here is to apply this

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:47.040
in conversations and Q&amp;A in the next few
days and make a habit out of answering

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:51.210
with short concise sentences. In our next
lesson, we're going to look at how to

00:07:51.210 --> 00:07:55.290
organize your thoughts clearly. I look
forward to seeing you then.

